steps.body:
A variable is a named box that holds one value. In Java every variable has a type, and you write it first: `type name = value;`. The type tells Java what kind of value the box may hold, and the compiler rejects the wrong kind, such as text in an `int`.

```java
int lives = 3;              // whole numbers
double speed = 4.5;         // numbers with a decimal point
boolean isReady = true;     // true or false
String nickname = "Ace";    // text in double quotes
```

`int`, `double`, and `boolean` are *primitive* types and start with a lowercase letter. `String` starts with a capital S because it is a class, not a primitive.

To change a variable, assign a new value without the type: `lives = 2;`. Writing the type again would declare a second variable with the same name, which is a compile error.

`System.out.println(lives);` prints the value, not the name. A whole `double` still shows its decimal point: `20.0` prints as `20.0`, never `20`. Very large doubles switch to E notation, so `12000000.0` prints as `1.2E7`. To label a value, print the label with `print` (no newline) and then the value with `println`:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int lives = 3;
        {{code}}
    }
}
```

```java
System.out.print("Lives: ");
System.out.println(lives);
```

Name variables in camelCase: start lowercase and capitalize each later word, as in `playerName` or `isOnline`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String playerName = "Nova";
        int level = 7;
        double health = 87.5;
        boolean isOnline = true;

        System.out.print("Name: ");
        System.out.println(playerName);
        System.out.print("Level: ");
        System.out.println(level);
        System.out.print("Health: ");
        System.out.println(health);
        System.out.print("Online: ");
        System.out.println(isOnline);

        level = 8;
        System.out.print("Level: ");
        System.out.println(level);
    }
}

