steps.body:
A value that never changes should be a constant. Declare it inside `Main` but above `main`, with `static final`:

```java
public class Main {
    static final int MAX_LIVES = 3;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(MAX_LIVES);
    }
}
```

`final` means it cannot be reassigned. `static` means it belongs to the class itself, so `main`, which is also `static`, can use it directly. Constants are named in UPPER_SNAKE_CASE.

`final` also works on a local variable inside `main`: `final int maxScore = 100;`. A final local keeps its camelCase name. Assigning to any final variable again, like `maxScore = 90;`, is a compile error: cannot assign a value to final variable maxScore.

`var` lets Java infer a local variable's type from its value: `var city = "Lisbon";` is a String and `var count = 12;` is an int. The type is still fixed, so `count = "twelve";` will not compile. `var` needs an initializer, because Java has nothing to infer from in `var total;`. `var` works only for local variables, so constants above `main` always name their type. Watch the literal too: `var price = 20;` makes an `int`, not a `double`. Use `var` when the type is obvious from the right side.

example.code:
public class Main {
    static final int MAX_PLAYERS = 4;
    static final double TAX_RATE = 0.08;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        final int maxScore = 100;
        var city = "Lisbon";
        var count = 12;

        System.out.print("Max players: ");
        System.out.println(MAX_PLAYERS);
        System.out.print("Tax rate: ");
        System.out.println(TAX_RATE);
        System.out.print("Max score: ");
        System.out.println(maxScore);
        System.out.print("City: ");
        System.out.println(city);
        System.out.print("Count: ");
        System.out.println(count);
    }
}

